  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Mind</p>
    <h1>Are Ravens Smarter Than Crows?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both ravens and crows are among the most cognitively capable birds known. Ravens score higher in some domains — future planning, gaze following, social cognition with large networks. Crows (especially New Caledonian Crows) are superior tool manufacturers. American Crows show stronger documented facial recognition. Neither species is definitively "smarter" — they are exceptional in different ways.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Do Exceptionally Well</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Key cognitive strengths documented specifically in ravens:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Future planning:</strong> Kabadayi & Osvath (2017) showed ravens select and save tools for tasks up to 17 hours in the future — matching great apes in controlled tests.</li>
      <li><strong>Social cognition:</strong> Ravens live in complex non-breeding flocks where they track individual relationships, alliances, and competitive histories across dozens of conspecifics.</li>
      <li><strong>Cache protection via theory of mind:</strong> Ravens re-cache food after being watched, perform false caching, and monitor observers' sight lines — suggesting awareness of others' knowledge states (Bugnyar & Heinrich, 2005).</li>
      <li><strong>Gaze following:</strong> Ravens follow human gaze around opaque barriers — suggesting they understand something about what others can and cannot see (Bugnyar et al., 2004).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Crows Do Exceptionally Well</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Key cognitive strengths in crows (various species):<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tool manufacture:</strong> New Caledonian Crows (<em>Corvus moneduloides</em>) fashion hook tools from leaves and sticks — the most complex tool manufacture known in birds. They show causal understanding of physical relationships in a way not demonstrated in ravens.</li>
      <li><strong>Facial recognition (controlled):</strong> American Crows show documented individual human face memory lasting at least 5 years in controlled experiments (Marzluff et al., 2010). No equivalent raven experiment has been published.</li>
      <li><strong>Urban problem solving:</strong> American and Carrion Crows have been documented using traffic to crack nuts, dropping shellfish onto rocks, and using water to soften food — creative solutions to urban problems.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why the Comparison Is Difficult</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Comparing "intelligence" between species is methodologically fraught:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Different research groups study different species using different methods — results are not directly comparable.</li>
      <li>Performance depends on the ecological relevance of the task. Ravens may outperform crows on social cognition tasks because their ecology demands more complex social navigation.</li>
      <li>Brain size relative to body mass is similar in ravens and large crows — both have relatively large brains for their body size among birds.</li>
      <li>The same population of ravens can vary substantially in task performance depending on experience, age, and social context.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The honest answer is: they are both remarkable, and the question "which is smarter?" is better rephrased as "what is each species good at, and why?"</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Questions</h2>
